Yes — you identified both parts correctly.

The first loop is **O(n)** and the nested loops are **O(n²)**. So together we get:

`O(n + n²)`

When Big-O terms are added, we keep the term that grows fastest. Since `n²` grows much faster than `n`, the overall complexity is:

**O(n²)**

So a good rule is: when different sequential sections have different complexities, the **largest-growing term dominates**.

For example, `O(n) + O(n²) + O(1)` becomes **O(n²)**.

